
export anchor DrawTheBoardSSidewaysScrollbarAsThe3:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        The board's sideways scrollbar is drawn very wrong. Once this is done, it is
        the same Scrollbar as everywhere else, turned on its side, and nothing
        about it is drawn as an upright column.

        Read these first: spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.description,
        spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.surface,
        spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.buttons,
        spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.track and
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout, which puts "the Scrollbar
        along its foot". The Scrollbar spec has nothing yet on a bar along a foot.
        It describes only a column along a list's right, so that part is new:
        add it to the Scrollbar spec as one more way the same scrollbar is laid out.

        What should be true:
        - Along the board's foot is one bar, 18 pixels tall and the full width of
          the board's scrolling area. It has the same surface as what it sits on,
          and runs the board's full width whether or not anything scrolls.
        - At its left end is a square button, 17 pixels wide and 18 tall, with an
          arrow pointing left. At its right end is the same button with an arrow
          pointing right. Each arrow is 6 pixels tall and 4 wide, centred up and
          down, 7 pixels in from the button's outer end, crisp on whole pixels,
          and in the same colour as the column's arrows. They lighten on hover and
          press as the column's buttons do.
        - The track runs between the buttons. A one-pixel line in the raised
          colour runs along both its top and its bottom edge, into the buttons, so
          the bar reads as one raised outline. No line runs up or down across it.
        - The thumb is a plain raised rectangle, the bar's full height, as wide as
          the share of the board in view and never too narrow to grab, placed as
          far along as the board is scrolled. It has a single column of pixels at
          its left and right ends in the thumb-end colour, and it lightens on hover
          and drag as the column's thumb does.
        - There is no lock button and no lock gap. Nothing from an upright column
          (its width, a horizontal line across it, an up or down arrow) appears
          anywhere in the bar.
        - It looks right in dark and light mode alike, on the board's surface.

        Fix it in src/scrollbar.rs. Every part the column draws should be laid out
        along the bar's axis, not with a fixed column width and height. Add a UI
        test that captures the sideways bar and checks the points above. It should
        check the bar pixel for pixel against the column's own parts turned on
        their side, so the two can't drift apart again.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            The board's sideways scrollbar is drawn very wrong. Once this is done, it is
            the same Scrollbar as everywhere else, turned on its side, and nothing
            about it is drawn as an upright column.

            Read these first: spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.description,
            spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.surface,
            spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.buttons,
            spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi#Scrollbar.track and
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout, which puts "the Scrollbar
            along its foot". The Scrollbar spec has nothing yet on a bar along a foot.
            It describes only a column along a list's right, so that part is new:
            add it to the Scrollbar spec as one more way the same scrollbar is laid out.

            What should be true:
            - Along the board's foot is one bar, 18 pixels tall and the full width of
              the board's scrolling area. It has the same surface as what it sits on,
              and runs the board's full width whether or not anything scrolls.
            - At its left end is a square button, 17 pixels wide and 18 tall, with an
              arrow pointing left. At its right end is the same button with an arrow
              pointing right. Each arrow is 6 pixels tall and 4 wide, centred up and
              down, 7 pixels in from the button's outer end, crisp on whole pixels,
              and in the same colour as the column's arrows. They lighten on hover and
              press as the column's buttons do.
            - The track runs between the buttons. A one-pixel line in the raised
              colour runs along both its top and its bottom edge, into the buttons, so
              the bar reads as one raised outline. No line runs up or down across it.
            - The thumb is a plain raised rectangle, the bar's full height, as wide as
              the share of the board in view and never too narrow to grab, placed as
              far along as the board is scrolled. It has a single column of pixels at
              its left and right ends in the thumb-end colour, and it lightens on hover
              and drag as the column's thumb does.
            - There is no lock button and no lock gap. Nothing from an upright column
              (its width, a horizontal line across it, an up or down arrow) appears
              anywhere in the bar.
            - It looks right in dark and light mode alike, on the board's surface.

            Fix it in src/scrollbar.rs. Every part the column draws should be laid out
            along the bar's axis, not with a fixed column width and height. Add a UI
            test that captures the sideways bar and checks the points above. It should
            check the bar pixel for pixel against the column's own parts turned on
            their side, so the two can't drift apart again.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I only had to add one sentence to the spec. Its Sideways section already described every point in your list: the 18-pixel bar, the 17×18 buttons, the left and right arrows 6 tall and 4 wide set 7 pixels in, the border along the top and bottom only, the full-height thumb with one-pixel end columns, and no lock button or lock gap. `piton check` and `piton build` both pass.

            The new sentence, in `spec/ui/components/scrollbar/index.pi`, says the bar's buttons, arrows, border, thumb and thumb ends are the column's own turned on their side, pixel for pixel, in dark and light mode alike. This is the rule your new UI test checks.

            For the code in `src/scrollbar.rs`, follow these anchors:
            - **`Scrollbar.sideways`**: the bar's layout and what must never appear in it.
            - **`Scrollbar.buttons`, `Scrollbar.track` and `Scrollbar.surface`**: the column parts and colours the bar transposes.
            - **`Planning.layout`**: puts the bar along the board's foot.
            \\\

    sentFrom: DrawTheBoardSSidewaysScrollbarAsThe2

    model: opus
